      "reasoning": "From g(f(x))<0, write t=f(x); then t^2-2t<0 gives 0<t<2. Since f(x)=2log_3(x/3), this is 3<x<9, so the integer candidates are 4,5,6,7,8. Also f(g(x)+3)=2log_3((x^2-2x+3)/3)<c is equivalent to x^2-2x+3<3^(1+c/2). At x=4,5,6,7,8 the left side is 11,18,27,38,51. Exactly four are below the threshold when 38<3^(1+c/2)<=51. For natural c, c=5 gives 3^(3.5)=27sqrt(3), which lies between 38 and 51; c=4 gives 27 and only two candidates, while c=6 gives 81 and all five. Thus the unique value is 5.",
